ch a double-flip is strategically poor.\n3.  **(F, 3)**: Similar to F4, this flips E4. It still leaves E5 as White, allowing the opponent to play C5 and flip D5 and D4.\n\n**Reason:**\nMove **(F, 5)** is the strongest choice. It secures a solid vertical structure and effectively blocks the opponent's potential strong counter-attack at C5. While the opponent can still play C5, it will only flip D4, whereas playing (F, 4) or (F, 3) would allow C5 to flip both D4 and D5, resulting in a significant loss of central control. F5 also keeps the play centrally focused within the 4x4 sweet spot.\n\nEvaluation: (F, 5) creates a strong vertical axis and mitigates the opponent's threat at C5 better than F4 or F3.\nReason: It flips E5, denying the opponent a double-flip opportunity at C5, and maintains central solidity.\nAnswer: (F, 5)"}
